  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix}$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det = \pm1$, $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{pmatrix} = $\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ -2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det = \pm1$, $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\ -c & a \end{pmatrix} = $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & -2 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 7 & -5 \\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\ 1 & -2 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 0 & 2 \\ 4 & -4 & -3 \\ -3 & -1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} -11 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -16 & -3 & 12 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 11 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 16 & 3 & -12 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -11 & 1 & -16 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 12 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} -11 & -2 & 8 \\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\ -16 & -3 & 12 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 & -1 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 0 & -2 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} -2 & -4 & 1 \\ 2 & 5 & -1 \\ 1 & 2 & -1 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1 & 1 \\ 3 & 4 & -3 \\ -3 & 4 & 4 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} -28 & -8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -24 & -7 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 28 & 8 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 24 & 7 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -28 & 3 & -24 \\\\ -8 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} -28 & -8 & 1 \\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\ -24 & -7 & 1 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 & -2 \\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\ 0 & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 & 2 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\ -2 & 3 & -2 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Find the inverse of $\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 4 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\ 2 & -1 & -3 \end{pmatrix}$ using the adjoint method $A^{-1} = \dfrac{1}{\det A}\operatorname{adj}(A)$.', '["$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -4 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & -11 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}$", "$\\begin{pmatrix} -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 11 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -5 & -4 \\end{pmatrix}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\det A = \pm1$, computing the matrix of cofactors, transposing to form the adjoint, and dividing by $\det A$ gives $A^{-1} = $\begin{pmatrix} -2 & 11 & -8 \\ -1 & 7 & -5 \\ -1 & 5 & -4 \end{pmatrix}$$, verified by $AA^{-1}=I$. [Source: Ch.1, pp.13-15 (“Inverse of a Matrix”)]', 'Inverse of a Matrix', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the lecture notes, what distinguishes a ''transcendental'' equation from an ''algebraic'' one?', '["A transcendental equation is one whose $f(x)=0$ is NOT reducible to a polynomial expression", "A transcendental equation always has no real roots", "A transcendental equation can only have positive roots", "There is no difference; the terms are interchangeable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "A polynomial equation... or an equation $f(x)$ where $f(x)$ is reducible to a polynomial expression is called an algebraic equation. If $f(x)=0$ is not reducible to an algebraic equation, we say that the equation is transcendental." [Source: Ch.2, p.23 (“Numerical Solution of Algebraic and Transcendental Equation”)]', 'Algebraic vs Transcendental Equations', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Which of the following is an example of a transcendental equation, as the term is used in the notes?', '["$x^3 - 2x + 1 = 0$", "$x\\cos x = 0$", "$2x^2 - 5x + 3 = 0$", "$x^4 - 1 = 0$"]'::jsonb, 1, '$x\cos x = 0$ involves the trigonometric function $\cos x$ and cannot be rewritten as a polynomial, so it is transcendental -- exactly the equation solved as Example 2 in the notes. The other three are all polynomial (algebraic) equations. [Source: Ch.2, p.23 (“Numerical Solution of Algebraic and Transcendental Equation”)]', 'Algebraic vs Transcendental Equations', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'What is a ''zero'' or ''root'' of the function $f(x)$?', '["A value of $x$ that satisfies $f(x) = 0$", "The value of $f(0)$", "The derivative of $f$ at $x=0$", "The y-intercept of any function"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "$x$ is a zero or root of the [f]unction $f(x)$ if $x$ satisfies $f(x) = 0$" -- and geometrically, this is where the graph of $f(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis. [Source: Ch.2, p.23 (“Numerical Solution of Algebraic and Transcendental Equation”)]', 'Algebraic vs Transcendental Equations', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'If $f(x) = g(x) - h(x)$, how does the notes describe finding a root of $f(x) = 0$ using $g$ and $h$?', '["A root of $f(x)=0$ is a point where the graphs of $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ meet (intersect)", "A root of $f(x)=0$ can never be found this way", "A root of $f(x)=0$ is where $g(x)$ equals zero, regardless of $h(x)$", "A root of $f(x)=0$ is the average of $g(x)$ and $h(x)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "if we can write $f(x) = g(x) - h(x)$ then a root of $f(x)=0$ is a point where the graph of $g(x)$ and $h(x)$ meet" -- since $f(x)=0$ means $g(x)=h(x)$. [Source: Ch.2, p.23 (“Numerical Solution of Algebraic and Transcendental Equation”)]', 'Algebraic vs Transcendental Equations', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For $f(x) = x^4 - 2x + 1$, the notes split this as $g(x) = x^4$ and $h(x) = -2x+1$ for the graphical method. What relationship must hold between $g$ and $h$?', '["$f(x) = g(x) + h(x)$", "$f(x) = g(x) - h(x)$", "$f(x) = g(x) \\times h(x)$", "$f(x) = g(x)/h(x)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With $g(x)=x^4$ and $h(x)=-2x+1$, $g(x)+h(x) = x^4 - 2x + 1 = f(x)$ -- the notes'' own worked example writes $\therefore f(x) = g(x)+h(x)$ for this particular split (since $h$ already carries the minus sign for the linear term). [Source: Ch.2, p.23 (“Numerical Solution of Algebraic and Transcendental Equation”)]', 'Algebraic vs Transcendental Equations', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'What is the general iterative formula for the Newton-Raphson method, as given in the lecture notes?', '["$x_n = x_{n-1} - \\dfrac{f(x_{n-1})}{f''(x_{n-1})}$", "$x_n = x_{n-1} + \\dfrac{f(x_{n-1})}{f''(x_{n-1})}$", "$x_n = x_{n-1} - f(x_{n-1}) \\cdot f''(x_{n-1})$", "$x_n = \\dfrac{x_{n-1}}{f''(x_{n-1})}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes derive and state: "Generally, $x_n = x_{n-1} - \dfrac{f(x_{n-1})}{f''(x_{n-1})}$. This is known as the Newton-Raphson process for the iterative solution." [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'The Newton-Raphson formula is derived in the notes from the slope of the tangent line at $P_1(x_1, f(x_1))$. Which trigonometric relationship is used in that derivation?', '["$\\tan(P_1\\hat{A}_2A_1) = f''(x_1)$, the slope of the curve at $P_1$", "$\\sin(P_1\\hat{A}_2A_1) = f(x_1)$", "$\\cos(P_1\\hat{A}_2A_1) = x_1$", "There is no trigonometric relationship used"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "$\tan P_1\hat{A}_2A_1$ is the slope of the curve at $P_1$, then $\tan P_1\hat{A}_2A_1 = f''(x_1)$" -- this is exactly what lets the notes equate $\dfrac{f(x_1)}{x_1-x_2}$ with $f''(x_1)$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, what is important to be aware of when choosing the first guess $x_1$ for the Newton-Raphson method?', '["The first guess must be reasonably close to the actual root, and one should be aware of any discontinuities in the graph", "The first guess can be any arbitrarily large number with no consequence", "The first guess must always be negative", "The choice of first guess never affects the outcome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes explicitly warn: "the first guess of a solution has to be reasonably close. It is also important to be aware of any discontinuities within the graph when making the first guess" -- illustrated by a diagram where a poor first guess near a discontinuity fails to converge to the intended root. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In Example 1 of the notes, the equation $x^2 - 27 = 0$ is solved by Newton-Raphson starting from $x_1 = 5$. What is $f''(x)$ for this function?', '["$f''(x) = 2x$", "$f''(x) = x$", "$f''(x) = 2$", "$f''(x) = x^2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For $f(x) = x^2 - 27$, differentiating gives $f''(x) = 2x$ -- exactly as stated in the notes'' solution: "$f''(x)=2x$." [Source: Ch.2, p.24-25 (Newton-Raphson worked Examples 1-2)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 1 ($f(x) = x^2-27$, $x_1=5$), $f(x_1) = -2$ and $f''(x_1) = 10$. What is the next approximation $x_2$?', '["$x_2 = 5.2$", "$x_2 = 4.8$", "$x_2 = 5.0$", "$x_2 = 5.4$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 5 - \left(\dfrac{-2}{10}\right) = 5 - (-0.2) = 5.2$, exactly the value found in the notes. [Source: Ch.2, p.24-25 (Newton-Raphson worked Examples 1-2)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Continuing the notes'' Example 1 ($x^2-27=0$), the iterations converge toward which value (to 3 decimal places)?', '["$5.196$", "$5.200$", "$5.000$", "$27.000$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes carry the iteration through $x_2=5.2$, $x_3=5.196$, and $x_4=5.196$ (same to 3 d.p.), concluding "there is no need continuing further... we have found a reasonable approximation." This matches $\sqrt{27}\approx5.196$. [Source: Ch.2, p.24-25 (Newton-Raphson worked Examples 1-2)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In Example 2 of the notes, the equation $x\cos x = 0$ is solved by Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x - \cos x$. What is $f''(x)$?', '["$f''(x) = 1 + \\sin x$", "$f''(x) = 1 - \\sin x$", "$f''(x) = -\\sin x$", "$f''(x) = \\cos x$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating $f(x) = x - \cos x$ gives $f''(x) = 1 - (-\sin x) = 1 + \sin x$, exactly as stated in the notes. [Source: Ch.2, p.24-25 (Newton-Raphson worked Examples 1-2)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 2 ($f(x)=x-\cos x$, $x_1=0.7$), the iterations converge to what value, correct to 3 decimal places?', '["$0.739$", "$0.700$", "$0.650$", "$1.644$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes compute $x_2=0.739$ and confirm $x_3=0.739$ to 3 d.p. as well, concluding "Hence $x=0.739$ to 3 decimal places." [Source: Ch.2, p.24-25 (Newton-Raphson worked Examples 1-2)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In Example 3 of the notes, $f(x) = 8x^3 - 24x^2 + 24x - 1.02$ is solved by Newton-Raphson from $x_1=0$. What is $f''(x)$?', '["$f''(x) = 24x^2 - 48x + 24$", "$f''(x) = 24x^2 + 48x - 24$", "$f''(x) = 8x^2 - 24x$", "$f''(x) = 3x^2 - 48x + 24$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term: $\frac{d}{dx}(8x^3)=24x^2$, $\frac{d}{dx}(-24x^2)=-48x$, $\frac{d}{dx}(24x)=24$, giving $f''(x)=24x^2-48x+24$ exactly as the notes state. [Source: Ch.2, p.25 (Newton-Raphson worked Example 3)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{2} - 16$ and first guess $x_1=5$, and given $f(x_1)=9$, $f''(x_1)=10$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$41/10$", "$51/10$", "$31/10$", "$5$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 5 - \dfrac{9}{10} = 41/10$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{2} - 50$ and first guess $x_1=7$, and given $f(x_1)=-1$, $f''(x_1)=14$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$99/14$", "$113/14$", "$85/14$", "$7$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 7 - \dfrac{-1}{14} = 99/14$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{3} - 8$ and first guess $x_1=3$, and given $f(x_1)=19$, $f''(x_1)=27$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$62/27$", "$89/27$", "$35/27$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 3 - \dfrac{19}{27} = 62/27$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{2} - 10$ and first guess $x_1=3$, and given $f(x_1)=-1$, $f''(x_1)=6$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$19/6$", "$25/6$", "$13/6$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 3 - \dfrac{-1}{6} = 19/6$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{3} - 27$ and first guess $x_1=4$, and given $f(x_1)=37$, $f''(x_1)=48$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$155/48$", "$203/48$", "$107/48$", "$4$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 4 - \dfrac{37}{48} = 155/48$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using Newton-Raphson with $f(x) = x^{2} - 20$ and first guess $x_1=4$, and given $f(x_1)=-4$, $f''(x_1)=8$, what is the next approximation $x_2$ (as a fraction or decimal)?', '["$9/2$", "$11/2$", "$7/2$", "$4$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_2 = x_1 - \dfrac{f(x_1)}{f''(x_1)} = 4 - \dfrac{-4}{8} = 9/2$, following the same formula used throughout the notes'' worked examples. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, what is a ''separation interval'' for a root $x_0$ of $f(x)=0$?', '["An interval $[x_1, x_2]$ containing only that one root of the equation", "Any interval containing all the roots of every possible equation", "The interval between two consecutive integers only", "An interval where $f(x)$ is always positive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "The interval $[x_1,x_2]$ is called a separation interval for the root $x_0$... One must ensure that the separation interval is as small as possible" and contains only the one root of interest. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the Method of Chord (regula falsi), the formula $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1) - x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1) - f(x_2)}$ is derived from what geometric idea?', '["The chord joining $P_1(x_1, f(x_1))$ and $P_2(x_2, f(x_2))$ crosses the $x$-axis at a point $x_3$ closer to the root $x_0$", "The tangent line to the curve at $x_1$", "The perpendicular bisector of the segment $P_1P_2$", "The midpoint of $x_1$ and $x_2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes explain: "the chord joining $P_1$ and $P_2$ crosses the $x$-axis... at a point $x_3$, which is closer to $x_0$ than either $x_2$ or $x_1$," and derive the formula from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'What is the key difference between the Method of Chord and the Newton-Raphson method, based on the lecture notes?', '["The Method of Chord uses two starting points that bracket the root (a separation interval), while Newton-Raphson uses only one starting point and the derivative", "The Method of Chord requires computing derivatives, while Newton-Raphson does not", "They are exactly the same method with different names", "The Method of Chord can only be used for quadratic equations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Newton-Raphson starts from one guess $x_1$ and repeatedly uses $f''(x)$ (a tangent line construction), while the Method of Chord starts from two points $x_1, x_2$ that bracket (separate) the root and repeatedly draws chords -- no derivative is needed. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 1 for the Method of Chord, applied to $8x^3-24x^2+24x-1.02=0$, the separation interval $(0,1)$ is checked to contain only one root by examining $f''(x)$. What is found?', '["$f''(x) = 24x^2-48x+24 = 24(x-1)^2$, which has $x=1$ as its only turning point, so there is only one zero in $(0,1)$", "$f''(x)$ has two turning points inside $(0,1)$, so there are three roots", "$f''(x)$ is never zero, so the function is a straight line", "The interval $(0,1)$ was found to contain no roots at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes factor $f''(x)=24x^2-48x+24=0$ as $24(x-1)^2=0$, giving the single turning point $x=1$; since there''s only one turning point, "there can be only one zero in $(0,1)$," justifying use of the Method of Chord on that interval. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' worked table for the Method of Chord (Example 1: $8x^3-24x^2+24x-1.02=0$), starting with $x_0=0$, $f(x_0)=-1.02$ and $x_1=1$, $f(x_1)=6.98$, the iterations $x_n$ converge toward which value?', '["$0.0444$", "$1.0000$", "$0.5000$", "$6.9800$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes'' table shows $x_n$ settling at $0.0444$ by the fourth and fifth rows (with $f(x_n)$ approaching zero), so this is the approximate root found by the Method of Chord. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the Method of Chord formula $x_{n+1} = x_0 - \dfrac{f(x_0)(x_0-x_n)}{f(x_n)-f(x_0)}$, what does $x_0$ represent throughout the iteration?', '["The fixed endpoint of the separation interval that is kept constant across every iteration", "A new value computed fresh at every step", "The final answer, computed before any iteration begins", "The derivative of $f$ at the root"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes present this as the "preferable" form of the formula, in which $x_0$ is one fixed endpoint of the original separation interval, reused in every iteration while $x_n$ updates -- distinguishing this fixed-point variant from the version where both endpoints update. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 30$ with $x_1 = 6$. What is $x_2$?', '["$11/2$", "$13/2$", "$9/2$", "$6$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 6$ and $f''(x_1) = 12$, so $x_2 = 6 - \dfrac{6}{12} = 11/2$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 40$ with $x_1 = 6$. What is $x_2$?', '["$19/3$", "$22/3$", "$16/3$", "$6$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -4$ and $f''(x_1) = 12$, so $x_2 = 6 - \dfrac{-4}{12} = 19/3$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 60$ with $x_1 = 8$. What is $x_2$?', '["$31/4$", "$35/4$", "$27/4$", "$8$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 4$ and $f''(x_1) = 16$, so $x_2 = 8 - \dfrac{4}{16} = 31/4$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 90$ with $x_1 = 9$. What is $x_2$?', '["$19/2$", "$21/2$", "$17/2$", "$9$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -9$ and $f''(x_1) = 18$, so $x_2 = 9 - \dfrac{-9}{18} = 19/2$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 15$ with $x_1 = 3$. What is $x_2$?', '["$23/9$", "$32/9$", "$14/9$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 12$ and $f''(x_1) = 27$, so $x_2 = 3 - \dfrac{12}{27} = 23/9$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 40$ with $x_1 = 3$. What is $x_2$?', '["$94/27$", "$121/27$", "$67/27$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -13$ and $f''(x_1) = 27$, so $x_2 = 3 - \dfrac{-13}{27} = 94/27$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 65$ with $x_1 = 4$. What is $x_2$?', '["$193/48$", "$241/48$", "$145/48$", "$4$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -1$ and $f''(x_1) = 48$, so $x_2 = 4 - \dfrac{-1}{48} = 193/48$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 100$ with $x_1 = 5$. What is $x_2$?', '["$14/3$", "$17/3$", "$11/3$", "$5$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 25$ and $f''(x_1) = 75$, so $x_2 = 5 - \dfrac{25}{75} = 14/3$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 12$ with $x_1 = 3$. What is $x_2$?', '["$7/2$", "$9/2$", "$5/2$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -3$ and $f''(x_1) = 6$, so $x_2 = 3 - \dfrac{-3}{6} = 7/2$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 70$ with $x_1 = 8$. What is $x_2$?', '["$67/8$", "$75/8$", "$59/8$", "$8$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -6$ and $f''(x_1) = 16$, so $x_2 = 8 - \dfrac{-6}{16} = 67/8$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 5$ with $x_1 = 2$. What is $x_2$?', '["$9/4$", "$13/4$", "$5/4$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -1$ and $f''(x_1) = 4$, so $x_2 = 2 - \dfrac{-1}{4} = 9/4$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} - 2$ with $x_1 = 1$. What is $x_2$?', '["$3/2$", "$5/2$", "$1/2$", "$1$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -1$ and $f''(x_1) = 2$, so $x_2 = 1 - \dfrac{-1}{2} = 3/2$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 3 x - 5$ with $x_1 = 2$. What is $x_2$?', '["$7/3$", "$10/3$", "$4/3$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -3$ and $f''(x_1) = 9$, so $x_2 = 2 - \dfrac{-3}{9} = 7/3$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{3} - 2 x - 5$ with $x_1 = 2$. What is $x_2$?', '["$21/10$", "$31/10$", "$11/10$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = -1$ and $f''(x_1) = 10$, so $x_2 = 2 - \dfrac{-1}{10} = 21/10$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = 2 x^{2} - 7$ with $x_1 = 2$. What is $x_2$?', '["$15/8$", "$23/8$", "$7/8$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 1$ and $f''(x_1) = 8$, so $x_2 = 2 - \dfrac{1}{8} = 15/8$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = 3 x^{2} - 20$ with $x_1 = 3$. What is $x_2$?', '["$47/18$", "$65/18$", "$29/18$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 7$ and $f''(x_1) = 18$, so $x_2 = 3 - \dfrac{7}{18} = 47/18$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = x^{2} + x - 20$ with $x_1 = 5$. What is $x_2$?', '["$45/11$", "$56/11$", "$34/11$", "$5$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 10$ and $f''(x_1) = 11$, so $x_2 = 5 - \dfrac{10}{11} = 45/11$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Apply one step of the Newton-Raphson formula $x_2 = x_1 - f(x_1)/f''(x_1)$ to $f(x) = 2 x^{3} - 9$ with $x_1 = 2$. What is $x_2$?', '["$41/24$", "$65/24$", "$17/24$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$f(x_1) = 7$ and $f''(x_1) = 24$, so $x_2 = 2 - \dfrac{7}{24} = 41/24$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = x^{3} - 5 x + 2$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$3 x^{2} - 5$", "$3 x^{2} + x - 5$", "$3 x^{2} - 6$", "$x^{3} - 5 x + 2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 3 x^{2} - 5$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = x^{4} - 3 x + 1$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$4 x^{3} - 3$", "$4 x^{3} + x - 3$", "$4 x^{3} - 4$", "$x^{4} - 3 x + 1$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 4 x^{3} - 3$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = 2 x^{3} - 6 x^{2} + 1$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$6 x^{2} - 12 x$", "$6 x^{2} - 11 x$", "$6 x^{2} - 12 x - 1$", "$2 x^{3} - 6 x^{2} + 1$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 6 x^{2} - 12 x$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = x^{2} - 4 x + 4$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$2 x - 4$", "$3 x - 4$", "$2 x - 5$", "$x^{2} - 4 x + 4$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 2 x - 4$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = x^{3} + 2 x^{2} - 5$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$3 x^{2} + 4 x$", "$3 x^{2} + 5 x$", "$3 x^{2} + 4 x - 1$", "$x^{3} + 2 x^{2} - 5$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 3 x^{2} + 4 x$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = 5 x^{2} - 3 x - 8$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$10 x - 3$", "$11 x - 3$", "$10 x - 4$", "$5 x^{2} - 3 x - 8$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 10 x - 3$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = x^{4} - 2 x^{2} + 3$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$4 x^{3} - 4 x$", "$4 x^{3} - 3 x$", "$4 x^{3} - 4 x - 1$", "$x^{4} - 2 x^{2} + 3$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 4 x^{3} - 4 x$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the Newton-Raphson method applied to $f(x) = 3 x^{3} - x + 7$, what is $f''(x)$?', '["$9 x^{2} - 1$", "$9 x^{2} + x - 1$", "$9 x^{2} - 2$", "$3 x^{3} - x + 7$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating term by term gives $f''(x) = 9 x^{2} - 1$. [Source: Ch.2, pp.23-24 (“Method of Tangents (Newton-Raphson Process)”)]', 'Newton-Raphson Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[4,5]$ for $f(x) = x^{2} - 20 = 0$, given $f(4)=-4$ and $f(5)=5$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$40/9$", "$49/9$", "$31/9$", "$4$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(5)(-4) - (4)(5)}{-4-5} = 40/9$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[3,4]$ for $f(x) = x^{3} - 30 = 0$, given $f(3)=-3$ and $f(4)=34$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$114/37$", "$151/37$", "$77/37$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(4)(-3) - (3)(34)}{-3-34} = 114/37$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[6,7]$ for $f(x) = x^{2} - 45 = 0$, given $f(6)=-9$ and $f(7)=4$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$87/13$", "$100/13$", "$74/13$", "$6$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(7)(-9) - (6)(4)}{-9-4} = 87/13$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[3,4]$ for $f(x) = x^{3} - 50 = 0$, given $f(3)=-23$ and $f(4)=14$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$134/37$", "$171/37$", "$97/37$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(4)(-23) - (3)(14)}{-23-14} = 134/37$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[2,3]$ for $f(x) = x^{2} - 8 = 0$, given $f(2)=-4$ and $f(3)=1$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$14/5$", "$19/5$", "$9/5$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(3)(-4) - (2)(1)}{-4-1} = 14/5$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[3,4]$ for $f(x) = x^{2} - 15 = 0$, given $f(3)=-6$ and $f(4)=1$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$27/7$", "$34/7$", "$20/7$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(4)(-6) - (3)(1)}{-6-1} = 27/7$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[2,3]$ for $f(x) = x^{3} - 20 = 0$, given $f(2)=-12$ and $f(3)=7$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$50/19$", "$69/19$", "$31/19$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(3)(-12) - (2)(7)}{-12-7} = 50/19$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Method of Chord with separation interval $[7,8]$ for $f(x) = x^{2} - 55 = 0$, given $f(7)=-6$ and $f(8)=9$, find the next approximation $x_3 = \dfrac{x_2f(x_1)-x_1f(x_2)}{f(x_1)-f(x_2)}$.', '["$37/5$", "$42/5$", "$32/5$", "$7$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$x_3 = \dfrac{(8)(-6) - (7)(9)}{-6-9} = 37/5$, following the chord formula derived in the notes from similar triangles. [Source: Ch.2, pp.25-27 (“Method of Chord”, Examples 1-2)]', 'Method of Chord', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In a linear programming problem, what is the ''objective function''?', '["The linear function $H = C_1x_1 + C_2x_2 + \\cdots + C_nx_n$ that is to be minimized or maximized", "One of the inequality constraints", "The number of variables in the problem", "A synonym for the feasible region"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "A linear programming problem requires that a linear function $H = C_1x_1 + C_2x_2 + \cdots + C_nx_n$ which is called objective function, be minimized or maximized subject to constraints." [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, how should a constraint written with $\geq$, such as $2x + 5y - 3z \geq 25$, be converted to the standard $\leq$ form used in the constraint matrix?', '["Multiply the inequality by $-1$, giving $-2x - 5y + 3z \\leq -25$", "Simply replace $\\geq$ with $\\leq$ without changing anything else", "Divide every term by $-1$ and keep the same inequality direction", "It cannot be converted; $\\geq$ constraints must be discarded"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes show exactly this conversion: "$2x+5y-3z \geq 25 \Leftrightarrow -2x-2y+3z \leq -25$... multiplying the inequality ($\geq$) by $(-1)$ to reser[v]e the sign of the inequality." [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, what is a ''feasible point''?', '["A point $(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ whose coordinates satisfy all of the problem''s constraints", "Any point in the plane, regardless of the constraints", "Only the point that maximizes the objective function", "A point where the objective function equals zero"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Definition from the notes: "A point $(x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n)$ is called feasible if it[s] coordinates satisfy all $m+n$ constraints." [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'If a variable $x$ in a linear program is allowed to take both positive and negative values (no restriction $x \geq 0$), the notes describe $x$ as:', '["Free", "Feasible", "Optimal", "Slack"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "Suppose that a variable $x$ can have both positive and negative values, we say that $x$ is free or we do not impose a restriction $x \geq 0$." [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the Simplex method as described in the notes, where does the solution to a linear programming problem lie?', '["Not only at boundary points, but at an extreme point of the constraint set", "Always strictly inside the feasible region, never on the boundary", "At the origin, regardless of the constraints", "At a randomly chosen feasible point"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "Simplex method, as we shall see, showed that a solution to the problem lies not only at the boundary points, but an extreme point of the constraint set," reducing the search to a finite number of extreme points. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = 2x_1 + 3x_2$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$10$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$3$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$9$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to3$, (2,2)$\to10$, (3,1)$\to9$, (3,0)$\to6$. The largest value is $10$ at $(2,2)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = x_1 + 5x_2$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$12$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$5$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$8$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to5$, (2,2)$\to12$, (3,1)$\to8$, (3,0)$\to3$. The largest value is $12$ at $(2,2)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = 4x_1 - x_2$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$12$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,0)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$-1$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$6$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to-1$, (2,2)$\to6$, (3,1)$\to11$, (3,0)$\to12$. The largest value is $12$ at $(3,0)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = x_1 + x_2$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$4$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$1$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$4$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to1$, (2,2)$\to4$, (3,1)$\to4$, (3,0)$\to3$. The largest value is $4$ at $(2,2)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = 3x_1 - 4x_2$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$9$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,0)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$-4$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$-2$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to-4$, (2,2)$\to-2$, (3,1)$\to5$, (3,0)$\to9$. The largest value is $9$ at $(3,0)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the feasible region of Example A in the notes (extreme points $(0,0)$, $(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$, $(3,0)$, from constraints $x_1\geq0$, $x_2\geq0$, $-x_1+2x_2\leq2$, $x_1+x_2\leq4$, $x_1\leq3$), find the maximum of $H = x_2 - 3x_1$ and the point where it occurs.', '["$1$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "$0$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,0)$", "$-4$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(2,2)$", "$-8$, at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $H$ at each extreme point: (0,0)$\to0$, (0,1)$\to1$, (2,2)$\to-4$, (3,1)$\to-8$, (3,0)$\to-9$. The largest value is $1$ at $(0,1)$ -- exactly the method the notes use in Example A (evaluating $F$ and $G$ at each extreme point). [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'A feasible region has extreme (corner) points $(0,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(0,3)$. Using the graphical method (evaluate the objective at every extreme point), find the maximum of $z = 2x + 3y$.', '["$9$ at $(x,y)=(0,3)$", "$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$8$ at $(x,y)=(4,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $z$ at each listed vertex gives the values 0, 8, 9; the largest is 9, occurring at $(0,3)$ -- the same vertex-evaluation technique demonstrated in the notes'' Example A. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'A feasible region has extreme (corner) points $(0,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(0,4)$. Using the graphical method (evaluate the objective at every extreme point), find the maximum of $z = 3x + 2y$.', '["$15$ at $(x,y)=(5,0)$", "$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$8$ at $(x,y)=(0,4)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $z$ at each listed vertex gives the values 0, 15, 8; the largest is 15, occurring at $(5,0)$ -- the same vertex-evaluation technique demonstrated in the notes'' Example A. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'A feasible region has extreme (corner) points $(0,0)$, $(6,0)$, $(0,2)$, $(4,2)$. Using the graphical method (evaluate the objective at every extreme point), find the maximum of $z = x + 4y$.', '["$12$ at $(x,y)=(4,2)$", "$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$6$ at $(x,y)=(6,0)$", "$8$ at $(x,y)=(0,2)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $z$ at each listed vertex gives the values 0, 6, 8, 12; the largest is 12, occurring at $(4,2)$ -- the same vertex-evaluation technique demonstrated in the notes'' Example A. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'A feasible region has extreme (corner) points $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(3,3)$, $(0,5)$. Using the graphical method (evaluate the objective at every extreme point), find the maximum of $z = 5x + y$.', '["$18$ at $(x,y)=(3,3)$", "$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$15$ at $(x,y)=(3,0)$", "$5$ at $(x,y)=(0,5)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Evaluating $z$ at each listed vertex gives the values 0, 15, 18, 5; the largest is 18, occurring at $(3,3)$ -- the same vertex-evaluation technique demonstrated in the notes'' Example A. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the Simplex method as taught in the notes, what is the purpose of introducing a ''slack variable'' to a constraint like $x_1 + x_2 \leq 4$?', '["It converts the inequality into an equation, $x_1 + x_2 + s = 4$, by adding a non-negative variable $s$ that absorbs the ''slack''", "It removes the variable $x_2$ from the problem entirely", "It changes the objective function''s coefficients", "It is only used for equality constraints, never inequalities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes'' Example 1 shows exactly this: "$x_1+x_2+s_2=4$" -- a non-negative slack variable is added to turn each $\leq$ constraint into an equation. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the Simplex tableau method described in the notes, how is the ''pivot column'' chosen from the objective function row?', '["By identifying a positive entry in the objective function row (when maximizing directly, without negating)", "By always choosing the leftmost column, regardless of sign", "By choosing the column with the smallest number in it", "The pivot column is fixed and never changes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the notes'' Example 1: "From the objective function row, we note that 1 is the only positive number present. This determines the pivot column" -- when maximizing directly (not using the negated-objective variant), a positive entry signals room for improvement. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Once a pivot column is chosen in the Simplex method, how is the pivot ROW selected, according to the notes?', '["By the minimum ratio test: dividing each row''s right-hand-side value by its (positive) entry in the pivot column, and choosing the row with the smallest such ratio", "By choosing the row with the largest right-hand-side value", "By always choosing the first row", "By choosing the row with the most negative entry"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes illustrate this directly: "In this column, we have two positive entries, but noting that $\frac{2}{2}=1$ is less than $\frac{4}{1}$, we make 2 a pivot [point]" -- the smaller ratio determines the pivot row. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'How does the notes describe recognizing that the Simplex method has reached the OPTIMAL solution (for a maximization problem, without negating the objective)?', '["When no entry in the objective function row is positive", "When every entry in the objective function row is positive", "When all slack variables equal zero", "After exactly three iterations, regardless of the tableau"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state directly: "Note that no entry in the objective function row is positive. This shows that the maximum of $H$... is equal to 1" -- optimality for a maximization tableau is reached once no positive entries remain in that row. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Simplex Example 1 (maximize $H = x_2 - x_1$), the final tableau $T_1$ has $-1$ as the entry in the bottom-right of the objective function row. What is the actual maximum value of $H$?', '["$1$", "$-1$", "$0$", "$2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes explain: "This shows that the maximum of $H = x_2 - x_1$ is equal to 1 i.e. negative of $(-1)$, which appears in the right hand side of the objective function row" -- the maximum is the negative of the value shown in that cell. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Simplex Example 2 (maximize $G = 2x_1 + x_2$, same constraints as Example 1), the final tableau shows $-7$ in the objective row''s RHS. Where does the maximum of $G$ occur, and what is its value?', '["Maximum $G=7$ at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$", "Maximum $G=-7$ at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,1)$", "Maximum $G=7$ at $(x_1,x_2)=(0,1)$", "Maximum $G=6$ at $(x_1,x_2)=(3,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes conclude: "the maximum of $G$ is negative of $(-7) = 7$. So, $G$ has maximum of 7 ... it occurs when $x_1=3, x_2=1$ as before" -- matching the graphical method''s result for the same objective. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, if the maximization problem''s objective function row is entered with NEGATED coefficients (as in Example 4, maximizing $z=18x_1+20x_2+32x_3$ using row $-18,-20,-32$), what determines the pivot column instead?', '["A negative entry in the objective function row (the notes look for negative entries, not positive ones, in this variant)", "A positive entry, exactly as in the non-negated variant", "The column is chosen at random", "The pivot column is always the last column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes explain: "Note that because we multiplied objective function by [-]1, we look for negative entries in the objective function row" -- the sign convention flips when the objective row itself is entered as negated coefficients. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 4, the final Simplex tableau has no negative entries in the objective row and shows the value 256. Since the objective row used negated coefficients, how is the maximum value of $z$ read from this final tableau?', '["Read directly as 256 (no sign flip needed once the stopping condition -- no negative entries -- is reached)", "It must be negated to $-256$", "It must be divided by the number of variables", "It cannot be determined from the tableau"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state plainly: "the maximum value of $z$ is 256... Note that the maximum is read as its is" -- once the negated-objective tableau has no negative entries left, the RHS value is the maximum itself, with no further sign change. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 4 final tableau, the optimal solution occurs at $x_1=0$, $x_2=8$, $x_3=3$. What is the maximum value of $z = 18x_1+20x_2+32x_3$, computed directly from these values?', '["$256$", "$246$", "$264$", "$224$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$z = 18(0) + 20(8) + 32(3) = 0 + 160 + 96 = 256$, matching the maximum value 256 read from the final tableau. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, if $x_0$ maximizes (or minimizes) $f(x)$ subject to the side condition $g(x)=0$, what relationship must hold between the gradients $\nabla f(x_0)$ and $\nabla g(x_0)$?', '["They must be collinear -- that is, $\\nabla f(x_0) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x_0)$ for some scalar $\\lambda$", "They must be perpendicular to each other", "They must both equal zero", "$\\nabla f(x_0)$ must equal $g(x_0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state Lagrange''s observation directly: "If $x_0$ maximizes (or minimizes) $f(x)$ subject to the side condition $g(x)=0$, then $\nabla f(x_0)$ and $\nabla g(x_0)$ are collinear. Consequently, there exists a scalar $\lambda$ such that $\nabla f(x_0)=\lambda\nabla g(x_0)$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Lagrange proof, why must $\nabla f(x_0)$ be perpendicular to the curve $C$ at $x_0$ if $x_0$ maximizes $f$ along $C$?', '["Because $\\dfrac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] = \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r''(t)$ must be zero at the maximum, forcing $\\nabla f(x_0)$ to be orthogonal to the tangent vector $r''(t_0)$", "Because all gradients are always perpendicular to every curve", "Because $f$ is assumed to be constant along $C$", "This relationship does not actually hold"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The proof in the notes shows that at a maximum/minimum along the curve, the derivative of the composition $f(r(t))$ must vanish, i.e. $\nabla f(x_0) \cdot r''(t_0) = 0$ -- and since $r''(t_0)$ is tangent to $C$, $\nabla f(x_0)$ must be perpendicular to $C$ there. [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 1 (maximize/minimize $f(x,y)=xy$ on the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$), the Lagrange condition $\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g$ gives $y=2\lambda x$ and $x=2\lambda y$. What key relationship between $x$ and $y$ does the notes derive from these?', '["$x^2 = y^2$", "$x = -y$ always", "$x + y = 0$", "$xy = \\lambda$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes multiply the first equation by $y$ and the second by $x$: "$y^2=2\lambda xy$, $x^2=2\lambda xy$... and thus $x^2=y^2$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Continuing the notes'' Example 1: with $x^2=y^2$ and the side condition $x^2+y^2=1$, what are the candidate values of $x$?', '["$x = \\pm\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}$", "$x = \\pm1$", "$x = 0$ only", "$x = \\pm2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Substituting $y^2=x^2$ into $x^2+y^2=1$ gives $2x^2=1$, so $x=\pm\sqrt{1/2}=\pm\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{2}$ -- exactly as stated in the notes. [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 1, $f(x,y)=xy$ takes the value $\frac{1}{2}$ at the maximum point and $-\frac{1}{2}$ at the minimum point on the unit circle. What are these two extreme values, in decimal form?', '["Maximum $0.5$, minimum $-0.5$", "Maximum $1$, minimum $-1$", "Maximum $0.5$, minimum $0$", "Maximum $1$, minimum $0$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "At the first and fourth points, $f$ takes on the value of $\frac{1}{2}$. At the second and third points, $f$ takes on the value $-\frac{1}{2}$. So, the first is a maximum and the second is a minimum." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 2 (minimize $f(x,y)=x^2+(y-2)^2$ on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1$), the Lagrange conditions simplify to $x=\lambda x$ and $y-2=-\lambda y$. Why does the notes conclude $\lambda=1$?', '["Because $x=\\lambda x$ combined with $x^2-y^2=1$ shows $x$ cannot be zero, so dividing $x=\\lambda x$ by $x$ gives $\\lambda=1$", "Because $\\lambda$ is defined to always equal 1 in the Lagrange method", "Because $y=2$ is given directly", "Because the hyperbola equation forces $\\lambda=1$ regardless of $x$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes reason: "The last equation shows that $x$ cannot be zero [since $x^2-y^2=1$ would fail]. So dividing $x=\lambda x$ by $x$ gives $\lambda=1$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Continuing the notes'' Example 2 with $\lambda=1$: the second Lagrange equation $y-2=-\lambda y$ becomes $y-2=-y$. What is $y$?', '["$y=1$", "$y=2$", "$y=0$", "$y=-1$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$y-2=-y \Rightarrow 2y=2 \Rightarrow y=1$, exactly as the notes state: "$y-2=-y$ and therefore $y=1$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 3 (maximize $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ subject to $x^3+y^3+z^3=1$, $x,y,z\geq0$), the Lagrange conditions lead to $x^3=y^3=z^3$ and thus $x=y=z$. Given the constraint, what is the value of each variable?', '["$x=y=z=(1/3)^{1/3}$", "$x=y=z=1/3$", "$x=y=z=1$", "$x=y=z=\\sqrt{3}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With $x=y=z$, the constraint $x^3+y^3+z^3=1$ becomes $3x^3=1$, so $x^3=1/3$ and $x=(1/3)^{1/3}$ -- exactly as the notes conclude: "$x=y=z=\left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^{1/3}$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 3, why is the possibility $\lambda=0$ explicitly excluded from the solution?', '["Because $\\lambda=0$ would force at least one of $x,y,z$ to be zero, making $xyz=0$ -- which the notes note \"is obviously not a maximum\"", "Because $\\lambda$ can never mathematically equal zero", "Because the constraint equation forbids $\\lambda=0$ directly", "There is no such exclusion in the notes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes reason: "We can exclude $\lambda=0$ because if $\lambda=0$ then at least one of the other variables would have to be [zero]. That would force $xyz$ to be zero, which is obviously not a maximum." [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Lagrange multiplier method (as in the notes'' Example 1), maximize $x+y$ subject to $x^2+y^2=4$. What is the maximum value?', '["$2*sqrt(2)$", "$2 + 2*sqrt(2)$", "$-2*sqrt(2)$", "$-1 + 2*sqrt(2)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting $\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g$ together with the constraint and solving the resulting system (exactly the technique in the notes'' Examples 1-2) gives the extreme values $-2*sqrt(2), 2*sqrt(2)$; the maximum is $2*sqrt(2)$. [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Lagrange multiplier method (as in the notes'' Example 1), maximize $2x+y$ subject to $x^2+y^2=5$. What is the maximum value?', '["$5$", "$7$", "$-5$", "$4$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting $\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g$ together with the constraint and solving the resulting system (exactly the technique in the notes'' Examples 1-2) gives the extreme values $-5, 5$; the maximum is $5$. [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Using the Lagrange multiplier method (as in the notes'' Example 1), maximize $x-y$ subject to $x^2+y^2=8$. What is the maximum value?', '["$4$", "$6$", "$-4$", "$3$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Setting $\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g$ together with the constraint and solving the resulting system (exactly the technique in the notes'' Examples 1-2) gives the extreme values $-4, 4$; the maximum is $4$. [Source: Ch.3, pp.33-35 (“The Lagrange Method”, Examples 1-3)]', 'Lagrange Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, in forming the dual of a maximum linear program, what happens to the roles of the objective-function coefficients $C$ and the constraint constants $B$?', '["Their roles are reversed: the constraint constants of one program become the objective-function coefficients of the other, and vice versa", "$C$ and $B$ remain in exactly the same roles in both programs", "Only $C$ is used in the dual; $B$ is discarded entirely", "$B$ is squared to form the dual''s objective function"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes'' "Primal Dual Connection" states: "The constraint constants of one program are the objective function coefficients of the other program" -- and separately note that "the roles of $C$ and $B$ have been reversed." [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the Primal-Dual Connection stated in the notes, how is the dual program''s constraint matrix related to the primal''s constraint matrix $A$?', '["The dual''s constraint matrix is the transpose $A^t$ of the primal''s matrix", "The dual uses the exact same matrix $A$, unchanged", "The dual''s constraint matrix is the inverse of $A$", "The dual has no constraint matrix at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "Note that the constraint[s] come from the transpose $A^t$ of matrix" and the Primal-Dual Connection confirms: "The constraint matrix is replaced by its transpose." [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'If a primal maximum program has $m$ constraints and $n$ variables, how many constraints and variables does its dual program have, according to the notes?', '["$n$ constraints and $m$ variables", "$m$ constraints and $n$ variables (unchanged)", "$m+n$ constraints and $m+n$ variables", "The dual always has exactly 2 constraints"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Primal-Dual Connection states: "if one program has $m$ constraints and $n$ variables, the other program will have $n$ constraints and $m$ variables" -- the roles swap. [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes, a maximum program is performed subject to which type of constraint, and a minimum program subject to which type?', '["Maximization is subject to $\\leq$ constraints; minimization is subject to $\\geq$ constraints", "Maximization is subject to $\\geq$ constraints; minimization is subject to $\\leq$ constraints", "Both use exactly the same constraint type", "Neither uses any inequality constraints"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes'' third Primal-Dual Connection rule states: "Maximization is performed subject to $\leq$ constraints and minimization is performed subject to $\geq$ constraints." [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'The notes find the dual of maximize $z=4x_1+7x_2$ subject to $x_1+5x_2\leq8$, $2x_1+3x_2\leq9$. What is the resulting dual objective function?', '["Minimize $U = 8w_1 + 9w_2$", "Minimize $U = 4w_1 + 7w_2$", "Maximize $U = 8w_1 + 9w_2$", "Minimize $U = 9w_1 + 8w_2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes compute: "The objective function is $U=WB=8w_1+8w_2$" [using the constraint constants 8 and 9] -- the dual objective''s coefficients (8, 9) are exactly the primal''s constraint constants, and it is a minimization since the primal was a maximization. [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' worked dual example (primal: maximize $4x_1+7x_2$ subject to $x_1+5x_2\leq8$, $2x_1+3x_2\leq9$), what are the dual''s constraints?', '["$w_1+2w_2 \\geq 4$ and $5w_1+3w_2 \\geq 7$", "$w_1+2w_2 \\leq 4$ and $5w_1+3w_2 \\leq 7$", "$4w_1+7w_2 \\geq 1$ and $2w_1+3w_2 \\geq 5$", "$w_1+5w_2\\geq8$ and $2w_1+3w_2\\geq9$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes give the dual constraints as "$W_1+2w_2 \geq 4$, $5w_1+3w_2\geq7$" -- formed from the transpose of the primal''s coefficient matrix, with the primal''s objective coefficients (4,7) now on the right-hand side. [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'According to the notes'' description of ''Solving Dual Programs'', where in the final Simplex tableau of a maximum program can the optimal solution to its DUAL program be found?', '["In the objective function row, under the slack variable columns", "In the right-hand-side (b) column only", "In the very first row of the initial tableau", "The dual solution can never be read from the primal''s tableau"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "the solution to the dual program has been available in the final simplex table of a maximum program. The solution of the dual program is located in the final table in the objective function row in the slack variables columns." [Source: Ch.3, pp.35-39 (“Duality of Linear Programming”, Examples 1-2, primal-dual connection)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'To solve a MINIMUM program with $\geq$ constraints using the Simplex method, what does the notes recommend as the first step?', '["Construct the corresponding maximum (dual) program with $\\leq$ constraints, using the primal-dual connection", "Immediately apply Newton-Raphson to the objective function", "Multiply every constraint by zero", "Minimum programs cannot be solved by the Simplex method at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes'' "Solving Minimum Programmes" section states: "If we are given minimum program with $\geq$ constraints, then we construct a maximum program with $\leq$ constraints such that the primal dual connection holds" as Step 1. [Source: Ch.3, pp.39-41 (“Solving Minimum Programmes”, Example 5)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 5 (minimize $U=8w_1+12w_2$ with $\geq$ constraints), the corresponding maximum program is solved by Simplex, giving the final tableau''s optimal solution as $w_1=3$, $w_2=3$. What is the minimum value of $U$?', '["$60$", "$96$", "$36$", "$24$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$U = 8(3)+12(3) = 24+36 = 60$, matching the notes'' conclusion: "we obtain $w_1=3$, $w_2=3$ as the optimal solution to the given minimum program and the minimum value is $u=60$." [Source: Ch.3, pp.39-41 (“Solving Minimum Programmes”, Example 5)]', 'Duality of LP', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Consider the constraint $x + y = 3$. According to the method shown in the notes for reducing all constraints to $\leq$ form, this is equivalent to which pair of $\leq$ constraints?', '["$x+y \\leq 3$ and $-x-y \\leq -3$", "$x+y \\leq 3$ only, with no second constraint needed", "$x-y\\leq3$ and $y-x\\leq3$", "$2x+2y\\leq6$ only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes work through exactly this: "$x+y=3 \Leftrightarrow x+y\leq3 \Leftrightarrow x+y\geq3 \Leftrightarrow x+y\leq3 \Leftrightarrow -x-y\leq-3$" -- an equality becomes two opposing inequalities, the second negated into $\leq$ form. [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the standard form $A\bar{x} \leq b$, $\bar{x}\geq0$ used throughout the notes, what does the constraint $x_j \geq 0$ (for each variable) represent?', '["Non-negativity: each decision variable cannot take a negative value, unless explicitly declared ''free''", "An upper bound of exactly zero on each variable", "A requirement that $x_j$ must equal zero", "A typo; the constraint should read $x_j \\leq 0$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes list "$x_j \geq 0$" as one of the standard constraints, then explain the exception: "Suppose that a variable $x$ can have both positive and negative values, we say that $x$ is free" -- so by default, variables are non-negative. [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Why does the notes multiply an inequality by $(-1)$ when converting a $\geq$ constraint to $\leq$ form?', '["Multiplying an inequality by a negative number reverses its direction, which is exactly what is needed to flip $\\geq$ into $\\leq$", "It has no effect on the inequality''s direction", "It only works for equations, not inequalities", "It changes the variables themselves, not just the inequality"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is standard inequality algebra used explicitly in the notes: "multiplying the inequality ($\geq$) by $(-1)$ to reser[v]e the sign of the inequality" -- negating both sides of an inequality reverses its direction. [Source: Ch.3, p.28 (“Introduction to Linear Programming”)]', 'LP Formulation', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(0,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(3,4)$, $(0,4)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = 3x + 5y$ over the region?', '["$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$29$ at $(x,y)=(3,4)$", "$3$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$-2$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 0, 15, 29, 20 is 0, occurring at $(0,0)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(0,0)$, $(6,0)$, $(4,3)$, $(0,5)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = 2x + y$ over the region?', '["$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$12$ at $(x,y)=(6,0)$", "$3$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$-2$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 0, 12, 11, 5 is 0, occurring at $(0,0)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(0,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(4,2)$, $(1,4)$, $(0,4)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = x + 2y$ over the region?', '["$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$9$ at $(x,y)=(1,4)$", "$3$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$-2$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 0, 4, 8, 9, 8 is 0, occurring at $(0,0)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(1,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(5,3)$, $(1,5)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = 4x + 3y$ over the region?', '["$4$ at $(x,y)=(1,0)$", "$29$ at $(x,y)=(5,3)$", "$7$ at $(x,y)=(1,0)$", "$2$ at $(x,y)=(1,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 4, 20, 29, 19 is 4, occurring at $(1,0)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(0,1)$, $(4,0)$, $(5,3)$, $(2,5)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = x + y$ over the region?', '["$1$ at $(x,y)=(0,1)$", "$8$ at $(x,y)=(5,3)$", "$4$ at $(x,y)=(0,1)$", "$-1$ at $(x,y)=(0,1)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 1, 4, 8, 7 is 1, occurring at $(0,1)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For the same feasible region with extreme points $(0,0)$, $(3,0)$, $(2,2)$, $(0,3)$, what is the MINIMUM value of $z = 6x + 5y$ over the region?', '["$0$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$22$ at $(x,y)=(2,2)$", "$3$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$", "$-2$ at $(x,y)=(0,0)$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The smallest of the vertex values 0, 18, 22, 15 is 0, occurring at $(0,0)$ -- the same vertex-checking technique used for maxima applies to minima. [Source: Ch.3, pp.28-30 (“Geometric View”, Example A)]', 'Graphical Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the Simplex tableau, the notes label the far-right column ''b'' or ''RHS''. What does this column represent?', '["The current values of the basic variables (right-hand sides of the equations after row reduction)", "The coefficients of the objective function", "The names of the slack variables", "The pivot column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Throughout the notes'' Simplex tableaux, the RHS/''b'' column carries the numeric values that the current basic variables equal -- e.g. in $T_0$ of Example 1, the column $[2,4,3]$ gives $s_1=2, s_2=4, s_3=3$ at the starting (origin) feasible point. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'At the very first tableau $T_0$ of a Simplex problem (before any pivoting), which point is typically chosen as the starting extreme feasible point, according to the notes?', '["The origin (all decision variables set to zero)", "The point that already maximizes the objective function", "A randomly chosen interior point", "The point where all slack variables equal zero"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state: "Since the origin is an extreme feasible point, we may choose $x_1=x_2=0$, $s_1=2, s_2=4$ and $s_3=3$ to start" -- decision variables at zero, slack variables absorbing the full right-hand sides. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 2 for the Simplex method (maximize $G=2x_1+x_2$), the initial objective row has BOTH 2 and 1 as positive entries. What does the notes say about this situation?', '["\"We have a choice\" -- either positive entry may be selected as the pivot column", "The problem is unsolvable when two positive entries exist", "Only the largest positive entry may ever be chosen", "The tableau must be restarted from scratch"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The notes state plainly: "Note that both 2 and 1 are positive in objective function row. So we have a choice," and proceed by selecting one of them (2) to continue. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'Once a variable such as $x_1$ becomes ''basic'' (enters the solution with a positive value) in the Simplex method, what happens to its column in the tableau after the pivot operation?', '["Every other entry in that column is eliminated to zero, leaving only a single 1 in the pivot row", "The entire column is deleted from the tableau", "The column''s values are all doubled", "Nothing changes in that column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is the standard row-reduction step the notes perform at every pivot: "making the pivot to be unity and then eliminating every other entry in its column" -- exactly as described for constructing $T_1$ from $T_0$ in Example 1. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'In the notes'' Example 3 (maximize $2x+3y$ with four $\leq$ constraints), four slack variables $s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4$ are introduced. Why does each of the four constraints need its own separate slack variable?', '["Because each inequality constraint is converted into its own separate equation, and each such equation needs a distinct non-negative slack variable to absorb its own ''leftover'' amount", "Because slack variables must always match the number of decision variables", "Because only one slack variable is actually needed, but four are added for style", "Slack variables are optional and were added by mistake"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each $\leq$ constraint becomes its own equation once a slack variable is added, and each equation''s slack variable is independent of the others -- the notes'' system shows $s_1$ through $s_4$, one per original constraint, exactly matching the four inequalities. [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2),
  ('mth-208-numerical-analysis', 'MTH 208: Numerical Analysis', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Mathematics', '200 Level', 'For a maximization problem solved by the (non-negated) Simplex method, once the final tableau is reached (no positive entries in the objective row), how is the optimal value of the objective function read off?', '["It is the value shown in the objective row''s right-hand-side (b) column of the final tableau", "It must be recomputed from scratch by substituting into the original objective function", "It is always equal to the sum of all the slack variable values", "It cannot be determined from the tableau at all"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Across the notes'' Examples 1-3 (non-negated objective row convention), the final answer -- e.g. "G has maximum of 7" -- is read straight from the objective row''s b-column entry (after accounting for the sign convention used in that particular setup). [Source: Ch.3, pp.30-33 (“The Simplex Method”, Examples 1-4)]', 'Simplex Method', false, 'second', 2);
